<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-2-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Nonlinear Models</title>

<assemblage component="instructor">
<p>This is the first Lesson on quadratic equations. Note that we start with extraction of roots, rather than the more traditional solution by factoring.</p>
<p>We recommend going over these Activities with the class, using a smart board or a projector. Almost all of the skills required will be new to most students. During class, take time to stress some of the basic ideas:</p>
    <p><ul>
        <li>How to recognize a quadratic equation, and to obtain the graph of the basic parabola.</li>
        <li>Solving an equation by graphing (in particular a quadratic equation).</li> 
        <li>The difference between an exact solution and a decimal approximation.</li>
    </ul></p>
<p>Point out that our first quadratic models include the Pythagorean theorem, formulas for volume and surface area, and the compound interest formula.</p>
<p>If there is time at the end of class, present some simple examples of solving other nonlinear equations (there are some in the Homework Problems)></p>

<p>GOALS</p>
<p><ul>
    <li><p>Recognize a quadratic equation and its parameters</p></li>
    <li><p>Graph a parabola by plotting points</p></li>
    <li><p>Solve a quadratic equation by extraction of roots</p></li>
    <li><p>Use formulas that involve quadratic terms</p></li>
</ul></p>
</assemblage>
  
<activity><title>Volume</title>

<introduction><p>You are designing a new coffee maker. The coffee filter must be 8.4 centimeters tall and shaped like a cone. The volume of the filter will therefore depend on how wide the opening is (the radius of the cone).</p></introduction>

<p><ol marker="a.">
    <li><p>Write a formula for the filter's volume <m>V</m> in terms of its widest radius <m>r</m> (at the top of the filter). (Hint: Look up the formula for the volume of a cone.)</p>
    <p>Simplify your formula by multiplying the constants together.</p></li>

    <li><p>Complete the table of values. Round the volumes to one decimal place.</p>
        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>r</m> (cm)</cell>
                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>4</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                    <cell>6</cell>
                    <cell>7</cell>
                    <cell>8</cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>V</m> (cm<m>^3</m>)</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular></li>

    <li><p>Look at the table of values. If you double the radius of the filter, by what factor does the volume increase?</p></li>
      <li><p>Write and solve an equation to answer the question: If the volume of the filter is 302.4 cubic centimeters, what is its radius?</p></li>
      <li><p>Use technology to graph the volume equation. (Use the table of values to help you choose a window.) Sketch your graph on paper, including scales on the axes). Label the coordinates of the point on the graph that corresponds to the filter in part (d).</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Extraction of Roots</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Solve each equation by extraction of roots.</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
            <li><p><m>(x+3)^2=4</m></p>
                <aside component="instructor"><p>Even students who recently completed elementary algebra have trouble with extraction of roots: although solving <m>x^2=16</m> is easy for them, solving <m>(x+3)^2=16</m> is not.</p></aside></li>
            <li><p><m>(3x+1)^2=25</m></p></li>
        </ol></p></li>

    <li><p>Solve. Give your answers two ways: (i) exact values and (ii) approximations rounded to thousandths.</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
            <li><p><m>6(x-3)^2=30</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>520+60(2+x)^2=1000</m></p></li>
        </ol></p></li>

    <li><p>Solve each formula for the requested variable.</p>
            <aside><p>2(3) Ask students how we should handle the fractions in these two formulas.</p></aside>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
            <li><p><m>V=\frac{1}{3}\pi r^2 h~~</m> for <m>r</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>d=h-\frac{1}{2}gt^2~~</m> for <m>t</m></p></li>
        </ol></p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Compound Interest</title>
<introduction><p>Cyril would like to invest $100 for two years in a money market account that pays interest compounded annually.</p>
</introduction>

<p><ol marker="a.">
    <li><p>Write a formula for the balance, <m>B</m>, of Cyril's account after two years in terms of the interest rate, <m>r</m>.</p></li>

    <li><p>Complete the table showing Cyril's account balance after two years for various interest rates.</p>
        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>r</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0.04</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0.06</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0.08</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0.10</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0.12</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>B</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular></li>

    <li><p>If Cyril would like to have $125 in two years, what interest rate must the account pay?</p>
         <aside component="instructor"><p>3c. Watch out for the mistake of solving <m>B=P(1+r)^2</m> by subtracting <m>P</m> from both sides.</p></aside></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Sketch a graph that illustrates why a quadratic equation can have two solutions.</p></li>
    <li><p>In part (d) of Activity 1, what technique did you use to solve the equation?</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain how to deal with the fractions in part (3) of Activity 2.</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain how you found the solution to part (c) of Activity 3, being careful to list all the steps you used.</p></li>
    
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Use graphs to explain why a linear equation can have only one solution, but a quadratic equation may have two solutions.</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain the difference between the volume and the surface area of a cylinder. Include units in your explanation.</p></li>
    <li><p>Which of these is an exact value:  <m>\sqrt{5}</m> or <m>2.236067977</m>?</p></li>
    <li><p>Delbert squared <m>-6</m> by entering <m>-6^2</m> into his calculator, and got <m>-36</m>. What went wrong?</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Which of the following equations cannot be solved by extraction of roots?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>2x^2+3=8</m></li>    
        <li><m>2x^2+3x=8</m></li>
        <li><m>2(x+3)^2=8</m></li>  
        <li><m>2x^2-3=0</m></li>
        </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>Which solutions are approximations?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>x^2=40,~~~~x= \pm 6.32455532</m></li>    
        <li><m>t^2 = \dfrac{81}{64},~~~~t= \pm 1.125</m></li>
        <li><m>w^2=50,~~~~w= \pm 5\sqrt{2}</m></li>    
        <li><m>b^2 = (0.632)^2,~~~~b= \pm 0.632</m></li>
        </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>What is the first step in solving the equation <m>~2(x+1)^2 = 17</m>?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>Expand <m>(x+1)^2</m>.</li>
        <li>Get zero on one side.</li>
        <li>Divide both sides by 2.</li>   
        <li>Take the square root of both sides.</li>
        </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>Which statement is true?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>A quadratic equation may include a linear or a constant term.</li>
        <li>The solutions of a quadratic equation are always of the form <m>\pm k</m>.</li>
        <li>Your calculator gives exact decimal values for square roots of integers.</li>
        <li>The coefficients of a quadratic equation are called parabolas.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>